            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0525" n="435" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg435"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 29/336: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wallace</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-09-11T21:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 11,
                                1920—9 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received September 11—4:22
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1685. B–242 for Davis, my B–238.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d368-f1"/></p>
                <p>Bradbury formula brought up rather unexpectedly to-day. Was accepted by everyone
                    after a specific explanation by Dubois and Bradbury that all rights of powers
                    not parties were reserved and arrangements of agreement were applicable only to
                    powers which signed. Result entirely satisfactory. Dubois expressed to me his
                    personal gratification at result my proposed protest which protest of course was
                    not presented. Boyden.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
